1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,000
Part 4. You will hear a lecturer on a languages course talking about the impact of digital technology on Icelandic, the native language of Iceland.

2
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:18,000
First, you have some time to look at questions 31 to 40.

3
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:32,000
Thank you.

4
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:14,000
Now listen carefully and answer questions 31 to 40.

5
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:18,000
Right everyone, let's make a start.

6
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:25,000
Over the past few sessions, we've been considering the reasons why some world languages are in decline.

7
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:36,000
And today I'm going to introduce another factor that affects languages, and the speakers of those languages, and that's technology, and in particular digital technology.

8
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:51,000
In order to illustrate its effect, I'm going to focus on the Icelandic language, which is spoken by around 321,000 people, most of whom live in Iceland, an island in the North Atlantic Ocean.

9
00:01:51,000 --> 00:02:01,000
The problem for this language is not the number of speakers, even though this number is small, nor is it about losing words to other languages such as English.

10
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:14,000
In fact, the vocabulary of Icelandic is continually increasing, because when speakers need a new word for something, they tend to create one, rather than borrowing from another language.

11
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:27,000
All this makes Icelandic quite a special language. It's changed very little in the past millennium, yet it can handle 21st century concepts related to the use of computers and digital technology.

12
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:36,000
Take for example the word for web browser. This is Vafri in Icelandic, which comes from the verb to wander.

13
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:42,000
I can't think of a more appropriate term, because that's exactly what you do mentally when you browse the internet.

14
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:49,000
Then there's an Icelandic word for podcast, which is too hard to pronounce, and so on.

15
00:02:49,000 --> 00:03:00,000
Icelandic then is alive and growing, but, and it's a big but, young Icelanders spend a great deal of time in the digital world, and this world is predominantly English.

16
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:15,000
Think about smartphones. They didn't even exist until comparatively recently, but today, young people use them all the time to read books, watch TV or films, play games, listen to music and so on.

17
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:28,000
Obviously, this is a good thing in many respects, because it promotes their bilingual skills, but the extent of the influence of English in the virtual world is staggering, and it's all happening really fast.

18
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:41,000
For their parents and grandparents, the change is less concerning because they already have their native speaker skills in Icelandic, but for young speakers, well, the outcome is a little troubling.

19
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:59,000
For example, teachers have found that playground conversations in Icelandic secondary schools can be conducted entirely in English, while teachers of much younger children have reported situations where their classes find it easier to say what is in a picture using English rather than Icelandic.

20
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:12,000
The very real and worrying consequence of all this is that the young generation in Iceland is at risk of losing its mother tongue.

21
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:22,000
Of course, this is happening to other European languages too, but while internet companies might be willing to offer, say, French options in their systems,

22
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:31,000
it's much harder for them to justify the expense of doing the same for a language that has a population the size of a French town, such as Nice.

23
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:38,000
The other drawback of Icelandic is the grammar, which is significantly more complex than in most languages.

24
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:43,000
At the moment, the tech giants are simply not interested in tackling this.

25
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:47,000
So, what is the Icelandic government doing about this?

26
00:04:47,000 --> 00:05:03,000
Well, large sums of money are being allocated to a language technology fund that it is hoped will lead to the development of Icelandic-sourced apps and other social media and digital systems, but clearly this is going to be an uphill struggle.

27
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:09,000
On the positive side, they know that Icelandic is still the official language of education and government.

28
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:19,000
It has survived for well over a thousand years, and the experts predict that its future in this nation state is sound and will continue to be so.

29
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:26,000
However, there's no doubt that it's becoming an inevitable second choice in young people's lives.

30
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:37,000
This raises important questions. When you consider how much of the past is tied up in a language, will young Icelanders lose their sense of their own identity?

31
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:53,000
Another issue that concerns the government of Iceland is this. If children are learning two languages through different routes, neither of which they are fully fluent in, will they be able to express themselves properly?

32
00:05:53,000 --> 00:06:00,000
That is the end of Part 4. You now have one minute to check your answers to Part 4.

33
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:25,000
Thank you.

34
00:06:53,000 --> 00:07:11,000
That is the end of the listening test. In the IELTS test, you would now have ten minutes to transfer your answers to the answer sheet.

